Public Key Infrastructure 1.4 

Key exchange without sending the key. This is Diffie-Hellman (often shortened to DH). The classic analogy is mixing paint:

Bob and Sally publicly agree on a starting colour, say yellow. Anyone can see this.
Each picks a secret colour they never share: Bob picks red, Sally picks blue.
Each mixes their secret colour with yellow and sends the result across. Anyone can see these mixes too.
Bob adds his red to Sally’s mix; Sally adds her blue to Bob’s mix. They both end up with the same final colour (yellow + red + blue).

An eavesdropper sees yellow and both mixes, but can’t “unmix” paint to work out the secret colours. So Bob and Sally now share a session key that never
travelled across the network.

2. Throwaway keys. This is perfect forward secrecy (PFS). You explained it well: each session uses a fresh temporary key (called an ephemeral key) that’s thrown 
away afterwards. If a server’s long-term private key is stolen next year, an attacker who recorded old traffic still can’t decrypt it, because those session keys
no longer exist anywhere.

Analogy: it’s like using a new combination padlock for every delivery and destroying it afterwards. Stealing the master key to the warehouse doesn’t open last year
’s boxes.

